.ank/** text eol=lf
crates/ank-core/tests/golden/** text eol=lf

# A log file is appended to and never rewritten, and two agents appending to
# one task's log on two branches is the ordinary case, not the exotic one. Git
# does not resolve that on its own: two lines added at the end of one file is
# the textbook adjacent-change conflict, and three texts in this repository
# claimed otherwise while nothing configured it. `union` takes both sides,
# which is exactly right for a file whose only operation is append and whose
# order carries no meaning a reader depends on -- every reader of a log sorts
# or reverses it itself.
#
# Declared for the directory rather than for `.ank/**`: an entity file has
# fields that cannot be unioned -- `version`, `status`, a frozen criterion --
# and unioning one would produce a file that parses into nonsense instead of a
# conflict somebody resolves. The log is the one shape where taking both sides
# is the right answer.
.ank/log/** merge=union

# The one exception, and it has to be one: this fixture's content *is* its line
# endings. Under the rule above git would hand it back in LF on every checkout
# and the CRLF test would pass while testing nothing. `-text` means no
# conversion in either direction -- the bytes committed are the bytes read.
crates/ank-core/tests/golden/valid/TASK-c71f0e5a9b23.md -text
